// Cyber Course content (written for Moundshroud): 11 modules, 34 lessons, each with takeaways, a link into the tools and a short quiz. Defensive and educational.
(() => {
  const Q = (q, a, c, e) => ({ q, a, c, e });
  const L = (id, title, min, html, take, tryit, quiz) => ({ id, title, min, html, take, tryit: tryit || [], quiz });
  const M = (id, icon, title, blurb, lessons) => ({ id, icon, title, blurb, lessons });
  const T_CODES = { label: 'Open the Code Breaker', go: 'codes' }, T_LAB = { label: 'Open the Cyber Lab', go: 'cyber' }, T_CHEF = { label: 'Open CyberChef', go: 'chef' };
  window.CYBER_COURSE = { title: 'Moundshroud Cyber Course', modules: [
    M('m1', '🧭', 'Welcome to cybersecurity', 'What it is, who is on the other side, and the rules for learning it safely.', [
      L('m1l1', 'What cybersecurity is', 8, `<p>Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting <b>information and the systems that hold it</b> from being stolen, changed, or made unavailable. Almost everything you do online depends on it: your bank, your messages, your photos, even the traffic lights.</p>
<p>Security people talk about the <b>CIA triad</b>, three goals that every defence serves:</p>
<ul><li><b>Confidentiality</b>: only the right people can read the information (passwords, encryption, access controls).</li><li><b>Integrity</b>: the information is accurate and has not been tampered with (hashes, digital signatures, backups).</li><li><b>Availability</b>: the system works when you need it (backups, redundancy, protection from denial-of-service attacks).</li></ul>
<p>A few words you will meet constantly: an <b>asset</b> is something worth protecting; a <b>vulnerability</b> is a weakness; a <b>threat</b> is something that could exploit it; and <b>risk</b> is how likely and how damaging that would be. The job is never to be perfectly safe, it is to <b>reduce risk to a level you can live with</b>.</p>`, ['Security protects confidentiality, integrity and availability.', 'Risk = how likely a threat is to use a weakness, and how bad it would be.', 'Perfect security does not exist; the goal is sensible risk reduction.'], [T_LAB], [
        Q('A hospital\'s patient records are encrypted so only doctors can read them. Which goal is mainly served?', ['Confidentiality', 'Integrity', 'Availability', 'Anonymity'], 0, 'Encryption keeps data readable only to those with the key: confidentiality.'),
        Q('A ransomware attack locks every computer in a company. Which goal is attacked?', ['Confidentiality', 'Availability', 'Authenticity', 'Privacy'], 1, 'Locked systems cannot be used, so availability is hit.'),
        Q('Which is a vulnerability?', ['An attacker in another country', 'A server that has never been updated', 'A phishing email', 'A stolen laptop'], 1, 'A vulnerability is a weakness. The others are threats or events.')]),
      L('m1l2', 'Who attacks, and why', 8, `<p>Understanding <b>motive</b> helps you predict what attackers will do. The main groups:</p>
<ul><li><b>Cybercriminals</b>: want money. Ransomware, fraud, stolen cards, selling stolen data. By far the most common threat to ordinary people and businesses.</li><li><b>Opportunists and beginners</b>: use ready-made tools against anything that looks weak. Basic hygiene stops most of them.</li><li><b>Insiders</b>: employees or partners who misuse access, on purpose or by accident. Accidents (wrong recipient, lost laptop) are far more common than malice.</li><li><b>Hacktivists</b>: want attention for a cause (defacing sites, leaking data).</li><li><b>Nation-state groups</b>: well funded, patient, interested in spying or disruption. Rare targets for most people, but they shape the tools everyone else uses.</li></ul>
<p>Most attacks are not clever. They follow a simple pattern sometimes called the <b>kill chain</b>: find a target, get in (often through a stolen password, a phishing email, or an unpatched system), look around, take what they came for, and cover their tracks. <b>Breaking the chain at any step</b> stops the attack, which is why layered defences work.</p>`, ['Most attackers want money, and most attacks are simple.', 'Insiders cause many incidents, usually by mistake.', 'Breaking any step of an attack\'s chain stops it.'], [], [
        Q('Which group is the most common threat to everyday users?', ['Nation-state spies', 'Cybercriminals after money', 'Cinema hackers', 'Hardware makers'], 1, 'Financially motivated criminals run the bulk of attacks.'),
        Q('Which cause is behind most insider incidents?', ['Deliberate sabotage', 'Honest mistakes', 'Foreign spies', 'Old hardware'], 1, 'Accidents like wrong recipients and lost devices are far more common.'),
        Q('Why do layered defences work?', ['Attackers give up when they see them', 'Each layer can break the attack chain', 'They make computers faster', 'They remove all vulnerabilities'], 1, 'Stopping an attack at any step defeats it, so more layers means more chances to stop it.')]),
      L('m1l3', 'Ethics, law and learning safely', 8, `<p>The skills in this course can be used to help or to harm. The line is <b>authorization</b>: you may only test systems that <b>you own, or that the owner has clearly allowed you to test in writing</b>. Probing someone else\'s website, network or accounts without permission is illegal in most countries, even if you mean no harm and even if you "only looked".</p>
<p>How to practise safely and legally:</p>
<ul><li>Use your <b>own devices</b> and a home lab (a spare computer or a virtual machine).</li><li>Use platforms built for learning: capture-the-flag (CTF) sites, intentionally vulnerable practice apps, and courses with their own labs.</li><li>If you ever find a real weakness in someone else\'s system, do not exploit it. Report it to the owner through their <b>responsible disclosure</b> or security contact page.</li><li>Never access data you were not given, even by accident; stop and report.</li></ul>
<p>Security is a profession built on trust. A clean record matters more than any skill.</p>`, ['Only test what you own or have written permission to test.', 'Practise in labs and CTFs, not on real sites.', 'Report weaknesses you find; never exploit them.'], [], [
        Q('You find a login page on a friend\'s website that you think is weak. What is the right move?', ['Try common passwords to prove it', 'Tell your friend and let them fix it', 'Post it online', 'Ignore the law since you mean well'], 1, 'Without permission, testing is illegal. Report it instead.'),
        Q('Where is it safe and legal to practise attack techniques?', ['Any website without a login', 'Your own lab and sites built for practice', 'Your school\'s network', 'A neighbour\'s Wi-Fi'], 1, 'Own labs and practice platforms give explicit permission.'),
        Q('What does "authorization" mean here?', ['A strong password', 'Clear permission from the owner to test', 'A security certificate', 'Admin rights on your PC'], 1, 'Authorization is the owner\'s permission, ideally in writing.')]),
    ]),
    M('m2', '💻', 'How computers work', 'The parts, the files, the command line and the logs you need to understand before you can secure them.', [
      L('m2l1', 'Hardware, operating systems and processes', 9, `<p>A computer has a <b>processor</b> (CPU) that runs instructions, <b>memory</b> (RAM) for what it is working on right now, and <b>storage</b> (SSD or hard drive) for what it keeps. The <b>operating system</b> (Windows, macOS, Linux, Android) manages all of this and decides which programs may do what.</p>
<p>Every running program is a <b>process</b>. The OS gives each its own memory and runs it under a <b>user account</b> with limited rights, so one broken program should not take down the rest. Programs ask the OS for help through <b>system calls</b> (open a file, send data over the network).</p>
<p>Why this matters for security: attackers want to run <i>their</i> code, usually under a powerful account. A lot of defence is about <b>keeping untrusted code from running</b> and <b>limiting what any program can touch</b>. Another key idea is the <b>boot process</b>: if something loads before the OS (firmware, a bootloader), antivirus inside the OS cannot see it, which is why Secure Boot exists.</p>`, ['RAM holds what is running, storage holds what is kept.', 'Each process runs under an account with limited rights.', 'Security often means stopping untrusted code from running with power.'], [], [
        Q('What is a process?', ['A file on disk', 'A running program', 'A network cable', 'A user password'], 1, 'A process is a program that is currently running.'),
        Q('Which part loses its contents when you switch the computer off?', ['SSD', 'RAM', 'Hard drive', 'USB stick'], 1, 'RAM is volatile.'),
        Q('Secure Boot helps because it…', ['Makes startup faster', 'Checks what loads before the operating system', 'Encrypts your files', 'Blocks all websites'], 1, 'It verifies early boot code so malware cannot hide below the OS.')]),
      L('m2l2', 'Files, users and permissions', 9, `<p>Every file and folder has an <b>owner</b> and <b>permissions</b> saying who may read, write, or run it. On Linux and macOS you will see them as letters (<code>rwx</code> for read, write, execute) for the owner, the group and everyone else. On Windows they are <b>access control lists</b> you edit in a file\'s Security tab.</p>
<p>Accounts come in two broad kinds: <b>standard users</b> and <b>administrators</b> (called <i>root</i> on Linux). An administrator can change anything, including security settings. The principle of <b>least privilege</b> says to use a powerful account only when you must. Browsing the web as an administrator means any malware you pick up gets administrator rights too.</p>
<p>Two habits that matter: <b>use a standard account for daily work</b>, and <b>never share one account between people</b>, because shared accounts make it impossible to tell who did what.</p>`, ['Permissions say who can read, write or run each file.', 'Least privilege: give only the access that is needed.', 'Use a standard account day to day; admin only when needed.'], [], [
        Q('A file shows permissions rwxr-xr-- . Who can write to it?', ['Everyone', 'Only the owner', 'The group', 'Nobody'], 1, 'The first triplet (rwx) belongs to the owner; the group has r-x and others r--.'),
        Q('Why browse as a standard user instead of an administrator?', ['It is faster', 'Malware would get fewer rights', 'It uses less memory', 'Websites require it'], 1, 'Whatever runs inherits the account\'s rights, so less privilege means less damage.'),
        Q('What is the main problem with a shared account?', ['It costs more', 'You cannot tell who did what', 'It is slower', 'It cannot be locked'], 1, 'Shared accounts destroy accountability.')]),
      L('m2l3', 'The command line', 10, `<p>The command line (Command Prompt, PowerShell, Terminal, bash) lets you control a computer with typed commands. Security people use it constantly because it is precise, scriptable, and works over remote connections.</p>
<p>Useful commands to recognise (safe, read-only):</p>
<ul><li><code>whoami</code>: which account am I?</li><li><code>ipconfig</code> (Windows) / <code>ip a</code> (Linux): my network addresses.</li><li><code>ping name</code>: is that host reachable?</li><li><code>dir</code> / <code>ls</code>: list files. <code>cd</code>: change folder.</li><li><code>tasklist</code> / <code>ps</code>: what is running.</li><li><code>netstat -an</code>: which network connections and ports are open.</li></ul>
<p>Be careful with any command you did not write: <b>copying a command from a website and pasting it into a terminal runs it with your rights</b>. Scammers use exactly this ("paste this to fix your PC"). Read it first, and if you do not understand it, do not run it.</p>`, ['The command line is precise and scriptable.', 'whoami, ipconfig/ip, ping, tasklist/ps and netstat show you what is going on.', 'Never paste a command you do not understand.'], [{ label: 'Open the Cyber Lab', go: 'cyber' }], [
        Q('Which command shows which network connections are open?', ['whoami', 'netstat -an', 'cd', 'dir'], 1, 'netstat lists connections and listening ports.'),
        Q('A web page tells you to paste a long command into PowerShell to "fix a virus". What should you do?', ['Paste it quickly', 'Read it and refuse to run what you do not understand', 'Run it as administrator to be safe', 'Share it with friends'], 1, 'Pasted commands run with your rights; this is a common scam.'),
        Q('Which is read-only and safe to try?', ['format c:', 'whoami', 'del *.*', 'shutdown /s'], 1, 'whoami only prints your account name.')]),
      L('m2l4', 'Logs: what your computer remembers', 8, `<p>Operating systems and programs keep <b>logs</b>: records of events such as logins, errors, software installs and network activity. Windows has the <b>Event Viewer</b> (Security, System, Application logs); Linux keeps text logs under <code>/var/log</code> and a journal you read with <code>journalctl</code>.</p>
<p>Logs are the main way to answer "what happened?" after something goes wrong. A few events are worth knowing: a <b>failed login</b> (Windows event 4625), a <b>successful login</b> (4624), a new <b>service or scheduled task</b> being created, and a <b>user account being added</b>. Many failed logins followed by a success is a classic sign of password guessing.</p>
<p>Two rules: <b>logs only help if they are kept and checked</b>, and <b>attackers try to delete them</b>, so important systems send logs to another machine.</p>`, ['Logs record events and are the memory of a system.', 'Many failed logins then a success suggests password guessing.', 'Keep logs, check them, and copy them somewhere safe.'], [], [
        Q('Which pattern suggests password guessing?', ['One successful login', 'Many failed logins then a success', 'A software update', 'A reboot'], 1, 'Repeated failures followed by success are a classic sign.'),
        Q('Why send logs to a second machine?', ['To save disk space', 'So an attacker cannot erase the evidence', 'To make them smaller', 'To speed up the PC'], 1, 'Local logs can be deleted by an intruder; remote copies survive.'),
        Q('Where does Windows let you browse logs?', ['Notepad', 'Event Viewer', 'Paint', 'Calculator'], 1, 'Event Viewer shows the Security, System and Application logs.')]),
    ]),
    M('m3', '🌐', 'Networking essentials', 'How data moves between computers, and where it can be watched or tampered with.', [
      L('m3l1', 'How data travels', 9, `<p>Data on a network is chopped into small <b>packets</b>. Each packet carries a header (where it is from and going to) and a piece of the data. Networks are described in <b>layers</b>, each with its own job: the physical link (cable, Wi-Fi), the network layer (IP addresses and routing), the transport layer (TCP or UDP, which add reliability or speed), and the application layer (web, email, DNS).</p>
<p><b>TCP</b> sets up a connection first (the "three-way handshake") and makes sure packets arrive in order. <b>UDP</b> just sends, which is faster and used for video calls and games.</p>
<p>Why it matters: every layer has its own attacks and its own defences. A <b>firewall</b> works mostly at the network and transport layers, <b>encryption</b> protects the application data inside the packets, and tools like Wireshark let defenders see packets to understand problems.</p>`, ['Data travels as packets, handled in layers.', 'TCP is reliable and connection-based; UDP is fast and simple.', 'Each layer has its own threats and defences.'], [], [
        Q('Which protocol sets up a connection with a handshake?', ['UDP', 'TCP', 'ARP', 'ICMP'], 1, 'TCP uses the three-way handshake.'),
        Q('A packet header mainly contains…', ['The user\'s password', 'Addressing and control information', 'The web page', 'A virus signature'], 1, 'Headers say where the packet came from and where it goes.'),
        Q('Why do networks use layers?', ['To confuse attackers', 'To split the job into manageable parts', 'To save electricity', 'Because cables are layered'], 1, 'Each layer solves one problem so the rest can be swapped independently.')]),
      L('m3l2', 'Addresses, ports and DNS', 9, `<p>Every device on a network has an <b>IP address</b> (like 192.168.1.20) and a hardware <b>MAC address</b> burned into its network card. Addresses starting 192.168, 10. or 172.16-31 are <b>private</b>: they only work inside your home or office, and your router uses <b>NAT</b> to share one public address between them.</p>
<p>A <b>port</b> is a numbered door on a device. A computer can run many services at once, each listening on its own port: web servers on 80 (HTTP) and 443 (HTTPS), SSH on 22, DNS on 53, email on 25 and 587. An <b>open port</b> is a service that is reachable, so every open port is something that must be kept updated and protected.</p>
<p><b>DNS</b> is the internet\'s phone book: it turns names like example.com into IP addresses. Because almost everything starts with a DNS lookup, attackers who can lie to your DNS can send you to a fake site. Using HTTPS and a trusted DNS provider (or encrypted DNS) reduces that risk.</p>`, ['IP addresses locate devices; ports locate services.', 'Private ranges and NAT keep home devices hidden behind one public address.', 'DNS turns names into addresses, so lying about DNS can misdirect you.'], [], [
        Q('Which is a private IP address?', ['8.8.8.8', '192.168.1.5', '142.250.1.1', '1.1.1.1'], 1, '192.168.x.x is a private range.'),
        Q('Which port normally carries HTTPS?', ['21', '25', '443', '3389'], 2, '443 is the standard HTTPS port.'),
        Q('What does DNS do?', ['Encrypts web pages', 'Turns names into IP addresses', 'Blocks viruses', 'Assigns passwords'], 1, 'DNS maps names to addresses.')]),
      L('m3l3', 'The web, HTTPS and TLS', 9, `<p>The web works by <b>requests and responses</b>: your browser asks a server for a page (<code>GET /</code>), the server answers with a status code (200 means OK, 404 not found, 403 forbidden, 500 server error) and the content. Plain <b>HTTP</b> sends all of this readable by anyone along the route.</p>
<p><b>HTTPS</b> wraps HTTP inside <b>TLS</b>, which does three things: it <b>encrypts</b> the traffic, it checks it was not <b>changed</b>, and it <b>proves who the server is</b> using a certificate signed by an authority your browser trusts. The padlock means the connection is encrypted and the certificate matches the name, <b>not that the site is trustworthy</b>. Scam sites use HTTPS too.</p>
<p>Habits: check the address (domain) before entering a password, do not ignore certificate warnings, and be wary of sites that only offer HTTP when they ask for any private information.</p>`, ['HTTP is readable by anyone on the path; HTTPS adds TLS encryption.', 'A certificate proves the name, not honesty. Scam sites have padlocks.', 'Read the domain and do not click through certificate warnings.'], [], [
        Q('What does the padlock on a web page guarantee?', ['The site is honest', 'The connection is encrypted to that domain', 'The site has no viruses', 'Your password is safe forever'], 1, 'It means encryption and a matching certificate, nothing about intent.'),
        Q('What does status code 403 mean?', ['Not found', 'Forbidden', 'OK', 'Server crashed'], 1, '403 is "forbidden".'),
        Q('TLS provides…', ['Encryption, integrity and server identity', 'Faster downloads', 'Free hosting', 'Spam filtering'], 0, 'Those three properties are the point of TLS.')]),
      L('m3l4', 'Wi-Fi, routers and firewalls', 9, `<p>Your <b>router</b> is the front door of your home network, so securing it matters most. Do these things: change the default admin password, keep the router\'s <b>firmware updated</b>, use <b>WPA2 or WPA3</b> encryption with a long Wi-Fi password, turn off <b>WPS</b> (an old convenience feature with weaknesses), and put guests and smart-home gadgets on a separate <b>guest network</b>.</p>
<p>A <b>firewall</b> allows or blocks traffic by rules (for example "allow replies to connections I started, block unexpected inbound connections"). Your router has one, and so does Windows. A firewall does not stop you running something harmful, but it greatly reduces what can reach you.</p>
<p>Public Wi-Fi: assume others can see unencrypted traffic and set up fake hotspots with familiar names. HTTPS protects most of what you do, but avoid banking on unknown Wi-Fi and consider a trusted VPN.</p>`, ['Change the router password, update its firmware, use WPA2/WPA3.', 'Put guests and gadgets on a separate network.', 'Treat public Wi-Fi as untrusted.'], [], [
        Q('Which Wi-Fi setting should you turn off?', ['WPA3', 'WPS', 'Guest network', 'Router firewall'], 1, 'WPS has known weaknesses.'),
        Q('What is a good reason for a guest network?', ['More speed', 'Keeps untrusted devices away from your main devices', 'Cheaper internet', 'Fewer passwords'], 1, 'Isolation limits what a compromised guest device can reach.'),
        Q('A firewall mainly…', ['Removes viruses', 'Allows or blocks traffic by rules', 'Speeds up Wi-Fi', 'Backs up files'], 1, 'It filters traffic according to rules.')]),
    ]),
    M('m4', '🔐', 'Cryptography', 'How secrets are kept, and how old ciphers get broken. Practise with the Code Breaker.', [
      L('m4l1', 'Ciphers and keys', 9, `<p><b>Cryptography</b> is the science of keeping information secret and verifying it. A <b>cipher</b> is a method for turning readable <b>plaintext</b> into scrambled <b>ciphertext</b>, using a secret <b>key</b>. <b>Kerckhoffs\'s principle</b> is the foundation of modern practice: a system should stay secure <b>even if everything about it is public except the key</b>. "Security through obscurity" (hiding how it works) is not security.</p>
<p>The oldest ciphers work on letters. A <b>Caesar cipher</b> shifts every letter a fixed number of places (A→D, B→E…), so a shift of 13 is ROT13. A <b>substitution cipher</b> swaps each letter for another by a fixed table. A <b>transposition cipher</b> keeps the letters but shuffles their order. These are fun to solve and teach the ideas, but none of them is safe today: a computer breaks them in a blink.</p>
<p>Try it: open the Code Breaker, type a sentence in <b>Caesar &amp; ROT</b>, shift it, then use <b>Brute force</b> to see all 25 possibilities at once. With only 25 keys, there is nothing to hide behind.</p>`, ['A key must stay secret; the method can be public (Kerckhoffs).', 'Caesar, substitution and transposition ciphers are easy to break today.', 'More possible keys means more work for an attacker.'], [T_CODES], [
        Q('A Caesar cipher has how many useful keys?', ['25', '26!', '1 million', '256'], 0, 'There are only 25 shifts, so brute force is trivial.'),
        Q('Kerckhoffs\'s principle says security should depend on…', ['Hiding the algorithm', 'Only the key being secret', 'A long message', 'A fast computer'], 1, 'A good system stays safe even if everything but the key is known.'),
        Q('A transposition cipher…', ['Replaces letters with symbols', 'Rearranges the letters', 'Shifts letters by a fixed amount', 'Hashes the text'], 1, 'Transposition changes order, not letters.')]),
      L('m4l2', 'Symmetric and asymmetric encryption', 10, `<p><b>Symmetric encryption</b> uses the <b>same key</b> to lock and unlock. It is very fast and is what actually encrypts your files and your web traffic. The standard is <b>AES</b>. The problem: how do two people share the key safely in the first place?</p>
<p><b>Asymmetric (public-key) encryption</b> solves that with a <b>pair of keys</b>. Anyone can lock a message with your <b>public key</b>, but only your <b>private key</b> can unlock it. It is slower, so it is mostly used to <b>agree on a symmetric key</b> and to make <b>digital signatures</b> (sign with the private key, anyone can verify with the public key). RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography are the common examples.</p>
<p>That is exactly what HTTPS does: public-key cryptography sets up a shared secret during the handshake, then fast symmetric encryption protects the rest. RSA\'s safety comes from a hard problem: multiplying two big primes is easy, but <b>factoring</b> the result is not. Try the <b>RSA playground</b> in the Code Breaker with small numbers to see why tiny keys fail.</p>`, ['Symmetric: one shared key, fast (AES).', 'Asymmetric: public key locks, private key unlocks, and signatures.', 'HTTPS uses both: public-key to agree, symmetric to encrypt.'], [T_CODES], [
        Q('In public-key encryption, which key do you keep secret?', ['The public key', 'The private key', 'Both', 'Neither'], 1, 'The private key must never be shared.'),
        Q('Why is symmetric encryption used for bulk data?', ['It is more secret', 'It is much faster', 'It needs no key', 'It cannot be broken'], 1, 'Symmetric ciphers like AES are very fast.'),
        Q('RSA relies on the difficulty of…', ['Guessing passwords', 'Factoring large numbers', 'Finding IP addresses', 'Compressing files'], 1, 'Factoring the product of two big primes is hard.')]),
      L('m4l3', 'Hashing, salting and signatures', 9, `<p>A <b>hash function</b> turns any input into a fixed-length fingerprint. The same input always gives the same hash, a tiny change gives a completely different one, and you <b>cannot run it backwards</b>. Hashes are used to check files were not changed (<b>integrity</b>) and to store passwords without keeping the passwords themselves.</p>
<p>Not all hashes are equal: <b>MD5</b> and <b>SHA-1</b> are broken for security uses (people can make two inputs with the same hash), while <b>SHA-256</b> is sound. But for <b>passwords</b> even SHA-256 is too fast: an attacker can try billions of guesses a second. Passwords need slow, purpose-built functions (<b>bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2</b>) plus a unique random <b>salt</b> per user, which stops precomputed tables and makes identical passwords hash differently.</p>
<p>A <b>digital signature</b> hashes a document and signs the hash with a private key; anyone can check it with the public key. It proves who signed it and that it was not altered. Try the <b>Hashes</b> tool: hash a word, then change one letter and compare.</p>`, ['A hash is a one-way fingerprint; changing one bit changes it all.', 'MD5 and SHA-1 are broken; passwords need bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2 with a salt.', 'Signatures prove who signed and that nothing changed.'], [T_CODES], [
        Q('Why add a salt when hashing passwords?', ['To make the hash shorter', 'So identical passwords get different hashes and tables do not work', 'To encrypt the hash', 'To remove the password'], 1, 'Salts defeat precomputed tables and hide duplicates.'),
        Q('Which is best for storing passwords?', ['MD5', 'SHA-1', 'Argon2', 'Base64'], 2, 'Argon2 is slow, salted and memory-hard.'),
        Q('Can you recover the original text from a hash?', ['Yes, by decrypting', 'No, it is one-way', 'Only with the public key', 'Only if it is short'], 1, 'Hashes are designed to be one-way (weak passwords can be guessed, not reversed).')]),
      L('m4l4', 'Breaking old ciphers', 11, `<p>Codebreakers do not guess keys at random, they use <b>patterns in language</b>. In English, E is the most common letter (about 12%), then T, A, O, I, N. A <b>simple substitution</b> cipher keeps those frequencies, just under different letters, so counting letters and matching them to English gives a start, and common pairs like TH and HE do the rest.</p>
<p>The <b>Vigenère cipher</b> was thought unbreakable for 300 years because it uses a repeating keyword, so the same plaintext letter becomes different ciphertext letters and the frequency pattern flattens. It fell to two ideas: finding the <b>key length</b> (by spotting repeats, the Kasiski method, or the <b>index of coincidence</b>, which is about 0.066 for English and about 0.038 for random text), then solving each position as a separate Caesar cipher.</p>
<p>Try the Code Breaker: <b>Frequency</b> shows the letter chart and index of coincidence, <b>Vigenère → Break it</b> finds the key length and key, and <b>Substitution</b> solves a scrambled alphabet automatically. Then take a few puzzles in <b>Practice</b>. The lesson of all this: every cipher that leaves a statistical pattern eventually loses.</p>`, ['Letter frequencies expose simple substitutions.', 'Vigenère falls once the key length is found (Kasiski, index of coincidence).', 'Modern ciphers are built to leave no usable pattern.'], [T_CODES], [
        Q('Which letter is most common in English text?', ['A', 'E', 'T', 'S'], 1, 'E is the most frequent English letter.'),
        Q('The index of coincidence of normal English is about…', ['0.038', '0.066', '0.5', '1.0'], 1, 'About 0.066, compared to about 0.038 for random letters.'),
        Q('What is the first step in breaking a Vigenère cipher?', ['Guess the plaintext', 'Find the key length', 'Hash the text', 'Count the words'], 1, 'Once you know the length, each position is a simple Caesar.')]),
    ]),
    M('m5', '🔑', 'Passwords and identity', 'Why passwords fail, how sites protect them, and what to use instead.', [
      L('m5l1', 'What makes a password strong', 8, `<p>Password strength is about how many guesses an attacker would need. <b>Length beats complexity.</b> A random 16-character password, or a passphrase of four to six random words, is far stronger than a short one full of symbols. Attackers do not guess letter by letter, they try <b>common passwords</b>, <b>leaked passwords</b>, and predictable tricks (Password1!, a name plus the year, a keyboard walk like qwerty).</p>
<p>Rules that work: <b>unique</b> (a different password for every account, so one breach does not spread), <b>long</b> (12+ characters, more for important accounts), and <b>not personal</b> (no pet names, birthdays, favourite teams). You cannot memorise dozens of unique passwords, which is why a <b>password manager</b> exists. Memorise one strong passphrase for the manager and let it generate and remember the rest.</p>
<p>Try the <b>Password lab</b> in the Cyber Lab to see how fast different passwords fall.</p>`, ['Length matters more than symbols.', 'Use a different password for every account.', 'A password manager makes this practical.'], [T_LAB], [
        Q('Which is stronger?', ['P@ssw0rd!', 'correct horse battery staple tulip', 'Summer2025', 'qwertyuiop'], 1, 'A long random passphrase beats short "clever" passwords.'),
        Q('Why must every account have a different password?', ['To make logins slower', 'So one leak does not unlock your other accounts', 'Because sites require it', 'To confuse hackers'], 1, 'Attackers reuse leaked passwords on other sites (credential stuffing).'),
        Q('What do attackers try first?', ['Every combination', 'Common and leaked passwords', 'Only 4-digit PINs', 'Your username backwards'], 1, 'Wordlists of common and leaked passwords are tried before brute force.')]),
      L('m5l2', 'How passwords get stolen', 9, `<p>Most stolen passwords never need clever hacking. The main routes:</p>
<ul><li><b>Data breaches</b>: a company is hacked and its user table leaks. If passwords were stored badly (plain text or fast unsalted hashes) many are recovered quickly.</li><li><b>Phishing</b>: you type your password into a fake page.</li><li><b>Credential stuffing</b>: automated tools try leaked email and password pairs on other sites.</li><li><b>Keyloggers and infostealers</b>: malware records what you type or steals saved browser passwords.</li><li><b>Guessing</b>: weak passwords on exposed services.</li><li><b>Shoulder surfing and reuse</b> by people you know.</li></ul>
<p>Defences map to each: unique passwords (stuffing, breaches), a password manager that only fills on the <i>real</i> domain (phishing), multi-factor authentication (all of them), and keeping the PC clean and updated (malware). Check whether an email address shows up in known breaches with a trusted breach-notification service, then change that password everywhere it was used.</p>`, ['Most stolen passwords come from breaches, phishing and reuse.', 'Credential stuffing tries leaked passwords on other sites.', 'MFA and unique passwords blunt almost all of these.'], [], [
        Q('What is credential stuffing?', ['Making passwords longer', 'Trying leaked logins on other sites', 'Encrypting a database', 'Resetting a router'], 1, 'Automated reuse of leaked credentials against other services.'),
        Q('A password manager helps against phishing because it…', ['Hides the page', 'Only autofills on the real domain', 'Blocks email', 'Makes passwords public'], 1, 'If the domain is fake, it will not fill, which is a warning sign.'),
        Q('Best response to finding your email in a breach?', ['Ignore it', 'Change that password everywhere it was used', 'Delete the email account', 'Buy antivirus'], 1, 'Reused passwords must be changed wherever they appear.')]),
      L('m5l3', 'Multi-factor authentication and passkeys', 9, `<p><b>Multi-factor authentication (MFA)</b> asks for something more than the password: something you <b>know</b> (password), <b>have</b> (phone, hardware key) or <b>are</b> (fingerprint, face). With MFA, a stolen password alone is not enough.</p>
<p>Not all second factors are equal. Best to worst: <b>hardware security keys and passkeys</b> (cannot be tricked into working on a fake site), <b>authenticator app codes</b> (good), <b>SMS codes</b> (better than nothing but can be intercepted by SIM-swapping), and <b>approve-prompts</b> that users accept without thinking (attackers spam them: "MFA fatigue").</p>
<p><b>Passkeys</b> replace the password with a cryptographic key pair stored on your device: the site keeps only your public key. There is nothing to phish or leak. Turn on MFA first for <b>email</b> (it resets everything else), then banking, cloud storage and social accounts. Keep <b>backup codes</b> somewhere safe.</p>`, ['MFA means a stolen password is not enough.', 'Security keys and passkeys beat app codes, which beat SMS.', 'Protect your email account first.'], [], [
        Q('Which second factor is hardest for phishing to defeat?', ['SMS code', 'Hardware security key or passkey', 'Security question', 'Email link'], 1, 'They are bound to the real site and cannot be replayed on a fake one.'),
        Q('What is MFA fatigue?', ['Being tired of passwords', 'Attackers spamming approval prompts until you accept', 'A weak battery', 'An old browser'], 1, 'Repeated prompts push users to approve by mistake.'),
        Q('Which account should get MFA first?', ['A game account', 'Your email', 'A shopping coupon site', 'A forum'], 1, 'Email resets passwords for everything else.')]),
    ]),
    M('m6', '🕸️', 'Web security', 'How web apps work and fail, so you can build and use them safely.', [
      L('m6l1', 'How web applications work', 9, `<p>A web app has a <b>front end</b> in your browser (HTML, CSS, JavaScript) and a <b>back end</b> on a server that runs code and talks to a <b>database</b>. HTTP is <b>stateless</b>: the server does not remember you between requests, so apps use <b>cookies</b> and <b>sessions</b>. When you log in, the server gives your browser a random session ID in a cookie, and your browser sends it with every request to prove it is you.</p>
<p>That session cookie is as good as your password while it lasts, which is why attackers want to steal it. Good cookies are marked <code>Secure</code> (HTTPS only), <code>HttpOnly</code> (JavaScript cannot read them) and <code>SameSite</code> (not sent on cross-site requests).</p>
<p>Golden rule of web security: <b>never trust anything from the browser.</b> Every form field, URL parameter, header and cookie can be changed by the user, so the server must <b>validate and authorize everything</b> itself.</p>`, ['Cookies and sessions are how a web app remembers you.', 'A stolen session cookie is like a stolen password.', 'Never trust input from the browser; check it on the server.'], [], [
        Q('Why do web apps use cookies?', ['To make pages pretty', 'HTTP is stateless and needs a way to remember you', 'To block ads', 'To speed up the internet'], 1, 'Cookies carry the session ID between requests.'),
        Q('What does the HttpOnly cookie flag do?', ['Forces HTTPS', 'Stops JavaScript reading the cookie', 'Deletes it after a day', 'Blocks all sites'], 1, 'It limits theft of the cookie by injected scripts.'),
        Q('Where must input be validated?', ['Only in the browser', 'On the server', 'Nowhere', 'In the database only'], 1, 'Browser checks are only a courtesy; users can bypass them.')]),
      L('m6l2', 'The common web flaws, in plain terms', 11, `<p>The <b>OWASP Top 10</b> is the best-known list of web risks. The big ideas, with the defence for each:</p>
<ul><li><b>Injection</b> (such as SQL injection): user input is mixed into a command, so the data becomes code. <i>Defence:</i> use <b>parameterized queries</b>, never build commands by joining strings.</li><li><b>Cross-site scripting (XSS)</b>: a site shows user-supplied text without escaping it, so an attacker\'s script runs in other visitors\' browsers. <i>Defence:</i> <b>escape output</b>, use a content security policy.</li><li><b>Broken access control</b>: the app checks you are logged in but not whether you may see <i>this</i> record (change ?id=5 to ?id=6 and read someone else\'s). <i>Defence:</i> check permission on <b>every</b> request on the server.</li><li><b>CSRF</b>: a malicious page makes your browser send a request to a site where you are logged in. <i>Defence:</i> anti-CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies.</li><li><b>Security misconfiguration</b>, <b>vulnerable components</b> (outdated libraries) and <b>weak authentication</b>: <i>Defence:</i> secure defaults, patching, MFA.</li></ul>
<p>Most of these come from one mistake: <b>trusting input</b>. Learn them to defend, and practise only on intentionally vulnerable labs, never on real sites.</p>`, ['Injection: keep data and commands apart with parameterized queries.', 'XSS: escape anything you display.', 'Broken access control: check permissions on every request.'], [], [
        Q('The best defence against SQL injection is…', ['Longer passwords', 'Parameterized queries', 'Hiding the database name', 'A bigger server'], 1, 'Parameterized queries keep user data from being interpreted as SQL.'),
        Q('Changing ?id=5 to ?id=6 and seeing another user\'s data is…', ['Normal', 'Broken access control', 'A firewall rule', 'Caching'], 1, 'The server failed to check you may see that record.'),
        Q('XSS is prevented mainly by…', ['Escaping output', 'Using GET not POST', 'Faster hosting', 'Longer URLs'], 0, 'Escaping stops user text running as script.')]),
      L('m6l3', 'Building and testing safely', 9, `<p>Defenders and developers follow a few habits that prevent most web problems: <b>validate input</b> on the server (what type, length and format is allowed), <b>escape output</b>, use <b>well-maintained frameworks</b> that handle the dangerous parts for you, <b>keep dependencies updated</b>, give the database account only the rights it needs, and never put <b>secrets</b> (passwords, API keys) in code or public repositories.</p>
<p>Testing is part of building. <b>Code review</b>, <b>automated scanners</b> and <b>penetration tests</b> find problems before attackers do. A penetration test is only legal when the owner has given <b>written authorization and a defined scope</b>. To learn the offensive side legally, use deliberately vulnerable practice applications and online labs, or run your own copies on your own machine.</p>
<p>When you find a real flaw in someone else\'s site, the responsible path is to <b>report it privately</b> to the owner (look for a security.txt file or a security contact page) and give them time to fix it.</p>`, ['Validate input, escape output, update dependencies, keep secrets out of code.', 'Penetration tests need written permission and a defined scope.', 'Report real flaws privately and responsibly.'], [T_LAB], [
        Q('Where should API keys and passwords be kept?', ['In the source code', 'In a public repository', 'Outside the code, in protected configuration', 'In a comment'], 2, 'Secrets in code get leaked; keep them in protected config.'),
        Q('A penetration test is legal when…', ['It is quick', 'The owner gives written authorization and a scope', 'The site has no login', 'You do it at night'], 1, 'Authorization and scope make it a test rather than an attack.'),
        Q('Why keep libraries updated?', ['They look newer', 'Old versions often have known flaws attackers use', 'Updates are free', 'It makes pages smaller'], 1, 'Known vulnerabilities in old components are a top cause of breaches.')]),
    ]),
    M('m7', '🎣', 'Malware and social engineering', 'The tricks that work on people and the software that does the damage.', [
      L('m7l1', 'Kinds of malware', 9, `<p><b>Malware</b> is software made to harm or spy. The common kinds:</p>
<ul><li><b>Viruses and worms</b>: spread themselves (worms across networks without help).</li><li><b>Trojans</b>: pretend to be something useful; you install them yourself. The most common way in.</li><li><b>Ransomware</b>: encrypts your files and demands payment to unlock them.</li><li><b>Spyware, keyloggers and infostealers</b>: record what you do and steal passwords, cookies and wallets.</li><li><b>Botnet clients</b>: quietly make your device part of an army used for spam or attacks.</li><li><b>Rootkits</b>: hide deep in the system to avoid detection.</li><li><b>Adware and PUPs</b>: unwanted programs that bundle with free software.</li></ul>
<p>Malware arrives through <b>email attachments and links, fake downloads and cracked software, malicious ads, infected USB sticks, and unpatched software</b>. Signs of infection: sudden slowness, programs you did not install, a browser with a new home page or extensions, antivirus turned off, or unexpected pop-ups. If you suspect infection, disconnect from the network, scan with a trusted tool, and change passwords from a <b>clean</b> device.</p>`, ['A trojan is the most common kind: you install it yourself.', 'Common sources: attachments, cracked software, bad ads, unpatched programs.', 'If infected: disconnect, scan, change passwords from a clean device.'], [], [
        Q('Which malware pretends to be a useful program?', ['Worm', 'Trojan', 'Rootkit', 'Botnet'], 1, 'A trojan is installed by the user, believing it is legitimate.'),
        Q('Ransomware…', ['Steals ads', 'Encrypts files and demands payment', 'Speeds up the PC', 'Only affects phones'], 1, 'It locks your data until you pay (which you should avoid doing).'),
        Q('First step if you suspect an infection?', ['Keep working', 'Disconnect from the network', 'Pay the attacker', 'Delete all files'], 1, 'Disconnecting stops spread and data theft.')]),
      L('m7l2', 'Phishing and scams: the anatomy', 10, `<p><b>Social engineering</b> attacks people instead of computers. <b>Phishing</b> is the best known: a message that pretends to be your bank, a delivery service, your boss or a friend, designed to make you click a link, open a file or give up a password. Its relatives: <b>smishing</b> (SMS), <b>vishing</b> (phone calls), <b>spear phishing</b> (aimed at one person), and <b>business email compromise</b> (a fake instruction from an executive to pay an invoice).</p>
<p>Almost every scam uses the same levers: <b>urgency</b> ("act now or your account closes"), <b>fear</b>, <b>authority</b>, <b>greed or generosity</b>, and <b>familiarity</b>. Checklist for any unexpected message:</p>
<ul><li><b>Who is it really from?</b> Look at the full sender address, not the display name. Lookalike domains swap letters (rn for m).</li><li><b>Where does the link go?</b> Hover without clicking, or type the site yourself.</li><li><b>What are they asking for?</b> Passwords, codes, gift cards, payments or remote access are red flags.</li><li><b>Verify another way:</b> call the number on the official site, not the one in the message.</li></ul>
<p>Slow down. Scammers rely on you reacting before thinking. Reporting a phish to your email provider or workplace helps protect others.</p>`, ['Scams use urgency, fear, authority and familiarity.', 'Check the real sender address and where links truly go.', 'Verify through a separate, trusted channel.'], [T_LAB], [
        Q('What is spear phishing?', ['Phishing by phone', 'A targeted message aimed at a specific person', 'A virus', 'A spam filter'], 1, 'It is personalised, which makes it more convincing.'),
        Q('A message says your account will close in one hour unless you click. This is…', ['Normal', 'A classic urgency tactic', 'A security update', 'A system error'], 1, 'Manufactured urgency is the scammer\'s main tool.'),
        Q('Best way to verify a suspicious request from your bank?', ['Reply to the message', 'Call the number on the bank\'s official website', 'Click the link', 'Ask a friend on social media'], 1, 'Use a channel you found yourself.')]),
      L('m7l3', 'Ransomware: prevention and response', 9, `<p>Ransomware encrypts files and demands money for the key. It usually arrives through a phishing attachment, a cracked program, or an exposed remote-access service with a weak password. Modern gangs also <b>steal the data first</b> and threaten to publish it ("double extortion"), so even backups do not solve everything.</p>
<p><b>Prevention</b>: keep systems patched, do not use cracked software, use MFA on remote access, restrict who can reach what, and keep <b>backups that ransomware cannot reach</b> (offline or immutable, following the <b>3-2-1 rule</b>: 3 copies, on 2 kinds of media, 1 kept off-site or offline). <b>Test restoring</b> from them.</p>
<p><b>If it happens</b>: disconnect the machine from the network (unplug the cable, turn off Wi-Fi) but do not power it off if you can avoid it, tell the people who need to know, do not pay in a hurry (payment funds crime and gives no guarantee), restore from clean backups after the system is rebuilt, and report it to the authorities. Backups are the real answer.</p>`, ['Ransomware arrives via phishing, cracked software and exposed remote access.', 'Backups must be offline or immutable; follow 3-2-1 and test restores.', 'If hit: disconnect, tell the right people, restore from clean backups.'], [], [
        Q('What does the 3-2-1 backup rule say?', ['3 passwords, 2 keys, 1 vault', '3 copies, 2 media types, 1 off-site or offline', '3 days, 2 weeks, 1 month', '3 disks in 1 PC'], 1, 'It spreads risk across copies, media and locations.'),
        Q('Why are always-connected backups risky?', ['They are slow', 'Ransomware can encrypt them too', 'They cost more', 'They use more power'], 1, 'Reachable backups can be encrypted or deleted by the attacker.'),
        Q('First action when ransomware is discovered?', ['Pay quickly', 'Disconnect from the network', 'Delete the files', 'Restart repeatedly'], 1, 'Stop it spreading to other devices and shares.')]),
    ]),
    M('m8', '🛡️', 'Defending systems', 'The layers that turn a soft target into a hard one.', [
      L('m8l1', 'Defence in depth and least privilege', 8, `<p>No single defence is perfect, so good security uses <b>layers</b> (defence in depth): if one fails, the next still protects you. Think of a castle with a moat, a wall, a gate, guards and a locked inner room. For a PC the layers might be: a firewall, updates, a standard user account, antivirus, encryption, backups, and an alert user.</p>
<p><b>Least privilege</b> means every person, program and account gets only the access it needs and nothing more, so a compromised account can do limited harm. Related ideas: <b>separation of duties</b> (no one person can do something critical alone), <b>need to know</b>, and <b>zero trust</b> ("never trust, always verify": being inside the network does not earn trust).</p>
<p>Prioritise by risk. For most people the order is: <b>updates, unique passwords with MFA, backups, careful clicking</b>. Those four stop the great majority of attacks and cost almost nothing.</p>`, ['Layers mean one failure does not mean a breach.', 'Least privilege limits damage from a compromised account.', 'Updates, passwords+MFA, backups and caution cover most risk.'], [], [
        Q('Defence in depth means…', ['One very strong defence', 'Several overlapping layers', 'Hiding the system', 'Buying more hardware'], 1, 'Layers cover each other\'s gaps.'),
        Q('Zero trust means…', ['Trust nobody ever', 'Verify every request, even inside the network', 'No passwords', 'No firewall'], 1, 'Location inside the network does not grant trust.'),
        Q('Which four habits cover most everyday risk?', ['Updates, unique passwords+MFA, backups, careful clicking', 'Faster internet, more RAM, bigger screen, a VPN', 'Paid antivirus only', 'Changing the wallpaper'], 0, 'Those basics stop most attacks.')]),
      L('m8l2', 'Patching and backups', 9, `<p>Most successful attacks use weaknesses that already have a fix. When a company announces a <b>patch</b>, attackers study it to find the hole and start attacking anyone who has not updated, sometimes within hours. <b>Turn on automatic updates</b> for the operating system, browser, and phone, and retire software that no longer receives updates.</p>
<p>Backups protect you from ransomware, hardware failure, theft, accidents and fire. A good habit is the <b>3-2-1 rule</b>: three copies of your data, on two different kinds of storage, one of them away from the computer (an external drive that is unplugged, or a trusted cloud copy with versioning). <b>Encrypt</b> backups that leave your home, and <b>test a restore</b> now and then, because a backup you have never restored is only a hope.</p>
<p>Also back up the things people forget: phone photos, password manager export (stored safely), recovery codes for important accounts, and documents on laptops.</p>`, ['Patches close known holes; attackers race you to use them.', 'Automatic updates are one of the best protections.', 'Test restores; an untested backup is only a hope.'], [], [
        Q('Why update quickly?', ['Updates are fun', 'Attackers exploit known holes soon after a patch is released', 'It deletes old files', 'It speeds up the router'], 1, 'Patch details help attackers target unpatched systems.'),
        Q('A good backup habit includes…', ['Never testing it', 'Occasionally restoring a file to check it works', 'Keeping it always plugged in', 'Using only one copy'], 1, 'Testing proves the backup is usable.'),
        Q('Which data is often forgotten?', ['Phone photos and recovery codes', 'Wallpaper', 'Browser history only', 'Temp files'], 0, 'Phones and recovery codes are easy to overlook but hard to replace.')]),
      L('m8l3', 'Hardening a PC and a home network', 10, `<p><b>Hardening</b> means removing unnecessary risk. A checklist for a PC: install updates, use a <b>standard account</b> for daily use, turn on the <b>firewall</b> and built-in antivirus (Windows Security is solid), enable <b>disk encryption</b> (BitLocker or Device Encryption on Windows, FileVault on macOS), set a screen lock, uninstall software you do not use, and keep browser extensions to a minimum.</p>
<p>For the home network: change the router password, update the router, use WPA2/WPA3, turn off WPS and remote administration, use a guest network for visitors and smart devices, and give <b>IoT gadgets</b> (cameras, plugs, TVs) unique passwords and updates. Buy devices from makers that publish security updates.</p>
<p>For accounts and phones: MFA everywhere it is offered, install apps only from official stores, review which apps have access to your camera, microphone and location, and keep a short list of <b>emergency contacts and recovery methods</b> offline.</p>`, ['Update, use a standard account, enable the firewall and disk encryption.', 'Router: new password, firmware updates, WPA2/WPA3, no WPS.', 'Give IoT devices their own network and updates.'], [], [
        Q('What does disk encryption protect against?', ['Malware downloads', 'Someone reading your data if the device is stolen', 'Slow internet', 'Phishing'], 1, 'If the drive is encrypted, a thief cannot read it without the key.'),
        Q('Where should smart-home gadgets go on your network?', ['Same network as your laptop', 'A separate guest or IoT network', 'Nowhere', 'On the router password'], 1, 'Isolation limits damage if a cheap gadget is compromised.'),
        Q('Which is hardening?', ['Installing many extensions', 'Removing software you do not use', 'Disabling updates', 'Sharing accounts'], 1, 'Less software means a smaller attack surface.')]),
    ]),
    M('m9', '🕵️', 'Detecting and responding', 'How defenders notice trouble, handle it, and learn from it.', [
      L('m9l1', 'Monitoring and indicators of compromise', 9, `<p>You cannot respond to what you cannot see. <b>Monitoring</b> collects logs and alerts from computers, networks and applications. Large organisations send them to a <b>SIEM</b> (security information and event management) system that correlates events and raises alerts; at home, built-in tools such as Windows Security and your router\'s log do a smaller version of the same job.</p>
<p><b>Indicators of compromise (IOCs)</b> are clues that something is wrong: an unknown program starting at boot, a new administrator account, logins from unusual countries or at odd hours, a sudden spike in outgoing data, strange scheduled tasks, or antivirus being switched off. A <b>baseline</b> of what is normal makes the abnormal stand out.</p>
<p>Detection is a balance: too many alerts and people ignore them (<b>alert fatigue</b>); too few and you miss real attacks. <b>False positives</b> waste time; <b>false negatives</b> are the dangerous ones.</p>`, ['A SIEM gathers logs and raises alerts.', 'IOCs: new admin accounts, odd logins, unexpected outgoing traffic.', 'Know what normal looks like so the abnormal is obvious.'], [], [
        Q('What is an indicator of compromise?', ['A software update', 'A clue that a system may be breached', 'A firewall rule', 'A backup'], 1, 'IOCs are evidence of possible intrusion.'),
        Q('Which is the more dangerous detection error?', ['False positive', 'False negative', 'Both are harmless', 'Neither exists'], 1, 'A false negative means a real attack was missed.'),
        Q('Alert fatigue is…', ['Slow alert delivery', 'Ignoring alerts because there are too many', 'A broken SIEM', 'A virus'], 1, 'Too many alerts lead people to tune them all out.')]),
      L('m9l2', 'The incident response lifecycle', 10, `<p>When something goes wrong, calm, ordered action beats panic. A widely used model has four phases (from the NIST guidance):</p>
<ol><li><b>Preparation</b>: plans, contacts, backups, logging, practice drills (before anything happens).</li><li><b>Detection and analysis</b>: confirm it is real, find out what is affected, and decide how serious it is.</li><li><b>Containment, eradication and recovery</b>: limit the spread (isolate machines), remove the cause (malware, stolen access, the vulnerability), then restore systems from clean sources and watch closely.</li><li><b>Post-incident activity</b>: write down what happened, what worked and what to change. A "lessons learned" review is where improvement comes from.</li></ol>
<p>Practical rules: <b>keep a timeline</b> of what you did and when, <b>change passwords from a clean device</b>, <b>preserve evidence</b> before wiping, <b>communicate</b> clearly to those affected, and <b>do not blame</b> the person who clicked: quick reporting matters more than perfection.</p>`, ['Preparation, detection, containment/recovery, then lessons learned.', 'Isolate first, then remove the cause, then restore from clean backups.', 'Keep a timeline and preserve evidence.'], [], [
        Q('Which phase comes first?', ['Containment', 'Preparation', 'Recovery', 'Lessons learned'], 1, 'Good outcomes depend on preparation done before an incident.'),
        Q('Why keep a timeline during an incident?', ['For fun', 'To understand what happened and when, and share it accurately', 'To delay recovery', 'It is required by Windows'], 1, 'A timeline guides decisions and the later review.'),
        Q('Why not blame the person who clicked?', ['It is rude only', 'Fear stops people reporting quickly, which makes things worse', 'It is illegal', 'They did nothing wrong ever'], 1, 'Fast reporting limits damage; blame discourages it.')]),
      L('m9l3', 'Digital forensics basics', 10, `<p><b>Digital forensics</b> is the careful study of digital evidence to find out what happened. Even at a basic level, a few principles apply. <b>Preserve</b> the original: work on a copy, because turning a machine on and poking around changes the evidence. Follow the <b>order of volatility</b>: collect the most fleeting evidence first (what is in RAM, running processes and network connections), then temporary files, then the disk, then backups and logs.</p>
<p><b>Chain of custody</b> is a written record of who handled the evidence and when, so it can be trusted later. Hash the copy (for example with SHA-256) and write the hash down; if it still matches later, nothing was altered.</p>
<p>Common artefacts: <b>file timestamps</b>, the <b>browser history and downloads</b>, the Windows <b>registry</b>, <b>prefetch</b> (programs that ran), event logs, and <b>deleted-but-not-overwritten</b> data. <b>Metadata</b> inside photos and documents (camera, GPS, author) can reveal a lot, which also matters for your own privacy when you share files.</p>`, ['Work from a copy and hash it so you can prove nothing changed.', 'Collect the most volatile evidence (RAM, connections) first.', 'Metadata and logs often tell the story.'], [T_CODES], [
        Q('What is the order of volatility?', ['Collect the most fleeting evidence first', 'Always start with the backups', 'Start with the oldest files', 'It does not matter'], 0, 'RAM and live connections vanish first, so capture them first.'),
        Q('Why hash an evidence copy?', ['To compress it', 'To prove later that it was not altered', 'To encrypt it', 'To rename it'], 1, 'If the hash still matches, the data is unchanged.'),
        Q('What is chain of custody?', ['A backup method', 'A record of who handled the evidence and when', 'A type of firewall', 'A file format'], 1, 'It keeps evidence trustworthy.')]),
    ]),
    M('m10', '🕶️', 'Privacy and everyday security', 'Practical habits that protect you, your family and your data.', [
      L('m10l1', 'What is tracked, and how to reduce it', 9, `<p>Most free services pay for themselves by <b>tracking you</b> and selling targeted ads. Trackers follow you with <b>cookies</b>, <b>browser fingerprinting</b> (your screen, fonts and settings make you recognisable), tracking pixels in emails, and the apps on your phone. Your <b>IP address</b> reveals roughly where you are, and big accounts (a search engine, a social network) link activity across devices.</p>
<p>Sensible steps that cost little: use a browser with <b>tracking protection</b> turned on, block third-party cookies, add one good <b>content blocker</b>, check each app\'s <b>permissions</b> (does a flashlight need your contacts?), switch off ad personalisation, and use separate email addresses for important accounts and for sign-ups. Read what you are agreeing to when an app asks for access to your photos or location.</p>
<p>Privacy is a spectrum, not an on-off switch: decide what you actually need to protect and choose habits you will keep.</p>`, ['Free services are often paid for by tracking.', 'Block third-party cookies and trackers; review app permissions.', 'Pick habits you will actually keep.'], [], [
        Q('Browser fingerprinting identifies you by…', ['Your fingerprint scan', 'Your browser and device settings', 'Your password', 'Your Wi-Fi name'], 1, 'The combination of screen, fonts and settings can be unique.'),
        Q('A simple flashlight app asks for your contacts. You should…', ['Allow it', 'Deny it or uninstall the app', 'Ignore it', 'Share it'], 1, 'The permission does not match the app\'s purpose.'),
        Q('Which is a low-cost privacy step?', ['Turning on tracker blocking and checking permissions', 'Switching off all updates', 'Using one password everywhere', 'Posting your location'], 0, 'Small settings changes reduce tracking noticeably.')]),
      L('m10l2', 'VPNs, Tor and encrypted messaging', 9, `<p>A <b>VPN</b> sends your traffic through an encrypted tunnel to a server, so your local network (and the café Wi-Fi) cannot read it and websites see the VPN\'s address instead of yours. What it does <b>not</b> do: make you anonymous (the VPN company can see your traffic), stop tracking by cookies or logins, or protect you from malware. Pick a provider you trust, not a random free one: free VPNs often make their money from your data.</p>
<p><b>Tor</b> routes traffic through several volunteer relays so no single point knows both who you are and where you are going. It gives strong anonymity for browsing, but is slower, and you can still reveal yourself by logging into personal accounts.</p>
<p>For private conversations use <b>end-to-end encrypted</b> messengers, where only the sender and recipient hold the keys and the service cannot read messages. Remember that backups, screenshots and the other person\'s phone can still leak what you say.</p>`, ['A VPN hides your traffic from the local network, not from the VPN company.', 'Tor gives stronger anonymity but is slower.', 'End-to-end encryption means the service cannot read your messages.'], [], [
        Q('What does a VPN NOT do?', ['Encrypt traffic on public Wi-Fi', 'Make you fully anonymous', 'Show websites a different IP address', 'Hide traffic from the café network'], 1, 'The VPN provider can still see your traffic, and cookies still track you.'),
        Q('End-to-end encryption means…', ['The company reads your messages', 'Only you and the recipient can read them', 'Messages never get delivered', 'Messages are deleted'], 1, 'Only the endpoints hold the keys.'),
        Q('Why be wary of free VPNs?', ['They are too fast', 'They may profit from your data', 'They have no apps', 'They block email'], 1, 'If it is free, you may be the product.')]),
      L('m10l3', 'Securing your accounts, devices and family', 10, `<p>Pull it together into a routine you can keep. <b>Accounts</b>: a password manager, unique passwords, MFA (email first), recovery options up to date, and a review once a year of which accounts you still use (close old ones). <b>Devices</b>: automatic updates, screen lock with a strong PIN or biometric, disk encryption, find-my-device turned on, and a backup.</p>
<p><b>Money and identity</b>: use a credit card or a payment service rather than a debit card online, turn on transaction alerts, and consider a <b>credit freeze</b> where available so nobody can open accounts in your name. Never give <b>one-time codes</b> to anyone who contacts you; legitimate staff never ask for them.</p>
<p><b>Family</b>: talk about scams openly, especially with older relatives and children. Agree on a <b>family code word</b> for emergencies so a cloned voice or a hacked message cannot fool you. Set up child-safe accounts and explain that anything posted can be copied. A calm, shared habit ("pause, check, ask") protects more than any tool.</p>`, ['Password manager, MFA, updates, backups: the core routine.', 'Never share one-time codes; consider a credit freeze.', 'Talk about scams and agree a family code word.'], [], [
        Q('Who may you give a one-time login code to?', ['Support staff who call you', 'Nobody', 'Your bank by SMS', 'Anyone who sounds official'], 1, 'Legitimate services never ask for it; that request means a scam.'),
        Q('What is a family code word for?', ['Wi-Fi password', 'Verifying an urgent request is really from family', 'Online games', 'Naming devices'], 1, 'It defeats voice cloning and hacked-account messages.'),
        Q('A credit freeze helps by…', ['Increasing credit limits', 'Stopping others opening new credit in your name', 'Hiding your email', 'Speeding up payments'], 1, 'Lenders cannot open new accounts without you lifting the freeze.')]),
    ]),
    M('m11', '🎯', 'Practice and careers', 'Where to practise legally, how the field is organised, and a plan for the next 30 days.', [
      L('m11l1', 'Practising legally: CTFs and labs', 9, `<p>The best way to learn is by doing, and there are plenty of legal places to do it. <b>Capture the flag (CTF)</b> competitions give you puzzles in categories such as <b>cryptography</b> (like the Code Breaker puzzles), <b>web</b>, <b>reverse engineering</b>, <b>binary exploitation</b>, <b>forensics</b> and <b>OSINT</b> (open-source research). Each solved challenge yields a hidden "flag" you submit for points. Many have beginner tracks and run all year.</p>
<p>Other options: guided learning platforms with browser-based labs, intentionally vulnerable practice applications you run on your own machine, free wargame sites that teach the Linux command line step by step, and your own <b>home lab</b> (a virtual machine you can break and rebuild). Bug bounty programs let you test real companies that <b>invite</b> you, but read each program\'s rules and scope carefully first.</p>
<p>Habits that make you better: keep notes of what you tried, <b>read other people\'s write-ups</b> after you solve or give up, and learn the basics (networking, Linux, a scripting language) before the flashy topics.</p>`, ['CTFs and labs are the legal way to practise attack skills.', 'Categories: crypto, web, reverse engineering, forensics, OSINT.', 'Keep notes, read write-ups, learn the fundamentals first.'], [T_CODES], [
        Q('What do you submit in a CTF challenge?', ['A report', 'A hidden flag', 'Your password', 'A screenshot of the desktop'], 1, 'The flag proves you solved it.'),
        Q('Which is a legal place to try attacks?', ['A random website', 'Your own virtual machine lab', 'A school server', 'A friend\'s router'], 1, 'Only systems you own or are invited to test are fair game.'),
        Q('What should you learn before advanced topics?', ['Nothing', 'Networking, Linux and scripting basics', 'Only reverse engineering', 'How to avoid logs'], 1, 'The fundamentals make everything else easier.')]),
      L('m11l2', 'Careers and certifications', 8, `<p>Cybersecurity is a large field with room for many personalities. Common roles: <b>security analyst</b> (watches alerts, investigates), <b>incident responder</b>, <b>penetration tester</b> (authorised attacker), <b>security engineer</b> (builds defences), <b>cloud security</b>, <b>application security</b>, <b>digital forensics</b>, <b>GRC</b> (governance, risk and compliance: policy and audits), and <b>security awareness and training</b>. Not everyone writes code; communication and curiosity matter a lot.</p>
<p>Well-known beginner and entry certifications include <b>CompTIA Security+</b> (general foundations), <b>CompTIA Network+</b> and <b>A+</b> (networking and hardware), and <b>GIAC</b> or <b>ISC2 SSCP</b> style programs for specific paths. Certifications help get interviews, but <b>projects, a home lab, CTF results and a clear write-up of what you learned</b> often matter just as much.</p>
<p>Start where your interest is: if you like puzzles, try crypto and CTFs; if you like helping people, awareness training; if you like building, security engineering; if you like investigating, forensics and incident response.</p>`, ['Many roles exist: analyst, responder, tester, engineer, GRC, forensics.', 'Security+ is a common entry certification.', 'Projects and write-ups strengthen a CV as much as certificates.'], [], [
        Q('Which role is an authorised attacker?', ['Auditor', 'Penetration tester', 'Receptionist', 'Help desk'], 1, 'Pen testers attack with permission to find weaknesses.'),
        Q('Which is a common general security certification?', ['CompTIA Security+', 'Driving licence', 'First aid', 'Pilot licence'], 0, 'Security+ covers foundational skills.'),
        Q('Which shows employers your skills besides certificates?', ['A home lab and written projects', 'Nothing else counts', 'A longer CV', 'A faster computer'], 0, 'Hands-on evidence is valued.')]),
      L('m11l3', 'Your 30-day plan', 8, `<p>Here is a simple plan to turn this course into habits. Adjust it to your pace.</p>
<ul><li><b>Week 1: Secure yourself.</b> Install a password manager, make your email password unique and strong, turn on MFA for email, banking and cloud storage, turn on automatic updates everywhere, and back up your important files using the 3-2-1 rule.</li><li><b>Week 2: Learn the foundations.</b> Revisit the networking and operating-system lessons, then try the Cyber Lab and the Command Prompt exercises.</li><li><b>Week 3: Break puzzles.</b> Use the Code Breaker: solve ten Practice puzzles across the levels, then try Auto-solve on a layered message you make yourself.</li><li><b>Week 4: Go further.</b> Join a beginner CTF, set up a virtual machine for a home lab, read two incident write-ups, and plan one certification or project.</li></ul>
<p>Finally, take the <b>Final exam</b> from the course home page. Scoring 80% or higher earns your certificate of completion. Keep going: security rewards curiosity, patience and ethics.</p>`, ['Week 1 secure yourself; week 2 foundations; week 3 puzzles; week 4 a CTF and a lab.', 'Make good habits first: MFA, updates, backups.', 'Finish with the final exam.'], [T_CODES, T_LAB], [
        Q('What should you do first?', ['Join a CTF', 'Secure your own accounts and backups', 'Buy a VPN', 'Study reverse engineering'], 1, 'Protect yourself first: it is quick and gives the biggest benefit.'),
        Q('Which account should get MFA first?', ['Email', 'A game', 'A forum', 'A shopping coupon'], 0, 'Email recovers every other account.'),
        Q('What is the final goal of the course?', ['Hack real sites', 'Build safe habits and skills ethically', 'Avoid all technology', 'Collect certificates only'], 1, 'Skills plus ethics and good habits.')]),
    ]),
  ] };
})();
